> (8)  "Everybody was satisfied with their grades."  Note the final "s".

"Everybody" is singular (think "each and every), so unless you're using
"their" as a third-person-neuter pronoun and each person had more than one
grade to be satisfied with, this is wrong.  It should be either "Everybody
was satisfied with her grade", or "Everybody was satisfied with their
grade", if you accept that 'their' can be singular.  "All were satisfied
with their grades" would probably be the best thing to do.

> (10) Even grammar nazis like me no longer use the word "shall" unless
> we're trying to be archaic or pedantic.

Why'd you become a grammar nazi if you can pass up the opportunity to be
archaic or pedantic?

I think some Brits still use it.  The Queen, anyway.

Fowler's explanation of the rules governing "will" and "shall" make it
pretty clear why no one uses them anymore.

http://www.bartleby.com/116/213.html

> (11) Yes, you shouldn't normally split infinitives, but in this
> particularly oft-ridiculed case, the cadence of the sentence becomes
> more important than strict textbook grammar.

I think split infinitives are a problem -- like sentences ending in
prepositions -- that only public school teachers born in the 19th century
care about.  And there's not many of them left.  It's still something worth
paying attention to, though, as it prevents a particular kind of
monstrosity ("To boldly, with heads held high and arms raised in salute of
the principles which have heretofore motivated our exertions, go" or the
like).  But "to boldy go" doesn't have that problem.

